{
  "image_name": "3d79c9dfb3e1b230c61617e5c9059031.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several nuclear RNA-surveillance routes that target abnormal or unwanted pre-mRNA species for degradation. Across the schemes, different classes of transcripts—promoter-associated RNAs, prematurely cleaved/polyadenylated RNAs, intron-containing transcripts, and readthrough RNAs—are recognized by distinct factors but often converge on a common outcome: poly(A) tail extension or short oligo(A) addition followed by destruction by the RNA exosome.\n\nA recurring theme is cooperation between cap-binding factors, splice-related proteins, and polyadenylation machinery. Complexes containing ZC3H14, PABPN1, and possibly PAXT or NEXT appear to mark faulty capped transcripts, promote hyperadenylation, and channel them to the exosome. One branch also highlights phosphorylation of ZC3H14 by CDK13. Restrictor-released and detained-intron RNAs are shown as additional exosome substrates, with some pathways drawn more tentatively. Another route suggests mTRAMP may act on certain intron-containing RNAs through oligoadenylation.\n\nThe bottom part shifts to transcriptional readthrough products. Capped readthrough pre-mRNAs seem to be processed either by decapping/XRN2 or by mTRAMP-assisted exosome decay, whereas uncapped readthrough RNAs are shown entering a DXO-dependent pathway. Overall, the image presents a network of parallel quality-control mechanisms that prevent accumulation of aberrant nuclear transcripts.",
